{#
    The creature's face.

    Included wherever the assistant appears with a face — the orb in the corner and the avatar docked
    in the panel header — so the two are literally the same markup rather than two that drift. The
    thinking indicator builds the same five elements in `assistant/face.js`, because it is created
    client-side at the moment a turn starts.

    Every visible shape sits inside a wrapper of fixed size. That looks like one element too many
    until an expression changes: a mood that widens an eye or turns it into an arc would shift its own
    centre if the shape were the positioned element, and the eyes would drift apart every time the
    creature laughed. The wrapper holds the anchor; the shape inside it is free to become anything.

    Sizing comes from `--swag-assistant-unit` on the host, so the same markup renders at 60px in the
    corner and 34px in the header with its proportions intact.
#}
{% block swag_assistant_face %}
    <span class="swag-assistant-face" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="swag-assistant-face__eye swag-assistant-face__eye--left">
            <i class="swag-assistant-face__pupil"></i>
        </span>

        <span class="swag-assistant-face__eye swag-assistant-face__eye--right">
            <i class="swag-assistant-face__pupil"></i>
        </span>

        {# Absent at rest, and every mood gives it a shape to grow into. This is where the Shopware
           signet used to sit; a mouth the creature can actually change is worth more there than a
           mark, and the widget runs in a merchant's storefront where our mark was never the point. #}
        <span class="swag-assistant-face__mouth">
            <i class="swag-assistant-face__lip"></i>
        </span>
    </span>
{% endblock %}
